[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c6859df-113d-54d9-873d-8def55780ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6859df-113d-54d9-873d-8def55780ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309720} :answer-field/id #uuid "9efd8fb1-640a-503e-961e-1f8575d430b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311251} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ada3950-77a3-534a-9126-2424ac176ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ada3950-77a3-534a-9126-2424ac176ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308959} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae0cb524-8bba-5c6f-8bc6-cd947713d578" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce3c7f2-3277-5749-9b74-cf88a88b0e03" :question/math-academy-id "q-338258" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2}=\\sqrt{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x\\le {360}^{∘},$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\sqrt{2} & =\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/14/144ed33e7739fbaf17e3f81dd9ae2fd0a2d8e4d622ced7cb5dd60c8d177b6c01.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x={135}^{∘}$ and $x={225}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ee027f-ab96-5007-9a3b-15da07c2b2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28ee027f-ab96-5007-9a3b-15da07c2b2ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309058} :answer-field/id #uuid "07f717dc-4c4f-5444-9b1e-ba8238060098" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc2e33c-cb6b-5501-99b9-47fb939d9356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bc2e33c-cb6b-5501-99b9-47fb939d9356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309075} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d76299e-c25c-5c70-b96d-4ec0f4b47e3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bd3a1f4-8a3e-58c2-8dac-3516d6de8f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338261" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4 & =1 \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/d1/d188a8634c7d33813260472c8691dccdbedbc809d3b59445afc912a43a09675b.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions of the equation, arranged in ascending order, are $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411f39e7-0823-5688-9bbd-b06dda423e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "411f39e7-0823-5688-9bbd-b06dda423e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309243} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ef731f-e3d0-5b92-a67a-aa6de7e866e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b47878c6-da2d-53a2-a437-6344bcce94de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b47878c6-da2d-53a2-a437-6344bcce94de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310210} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3296c46-a9b2-5573-9558-6ef673eb3d32" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72045dd8-ca49-595c-a601-e4817421c93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-338342" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{2}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)=1.281\\,044…≈1.2810\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.2810$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=1.3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.2810.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b1b50c4d13ebae81a4c8af45ec2c9fde8dc6810da78bd21f74ebcdd117a375e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.2810 \\\\  & ≈5.0022 \\\\  & ≈5.0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=1.3$ and $x=5.0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f4dfd6-f6b1-5798-b8b2-ac5c66a081f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f4dfd6-f6b1-5798-b8b2-ac5c66a081f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308939} :answer-field/id #uuid "0edbdab2-7ae2-55af-b0fd-d292c34d8ac2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd68e334-5137-5bb1-ba69-4cd2d9f11627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd68e334-5137-5bb1-ba69-4cd2d9f11627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310777} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ecb639-f17f-5d21-95e9-441eda8c4e05" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60c9101d-4b05-5101-8e3d-9f6a1a089c95" :question/math-academy-id "q-338346" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **one decimal place**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{7}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right)=2.013\\,707…≈2.0137\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.0137$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is $x=2.0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.0137≈1.1279.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.1279.$\n\n![](images/15/157096afa9d3de44710dfa11c99f1f699815156fbcd5d649c1b4048dbbba9913.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.1279 \\\\  & ≈4.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2.0$ and $x=4.3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f98eac75-1aff-5088-a576-c59b158047fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f98eac75-1aff-5088-a576-c59b158047fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310737} :answer-field/id #uuid "2efcb036-aee4-5880-a65a-13bba3da1494" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310917} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a6fed05-3cde-552b-be39-1f0d3a9cdfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6fed05-3cde-552b-be39-1f0d3a9cdfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309574} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5945fe-824b-5308-9857-ca20e2f79d7f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "001033a3-e0b0-54ac-ae25-4f7b5431eaa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-338422" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{9}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{4}{9}\\right)=2.031\\,350…≈2.031\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.031$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of \n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -2.031≈1.111.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.111.$\n\n![](images/d4/d416faad39ff978c5ad59b91ceee311d358818ee7bf486f0dea95b9ae6319978.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.111 \\\\  & ≈4.253 \\\\  & ≈4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4a08bf6-bbd5-5c7a-a458-cc2f28795f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450518} {:answer/id #uuid "bc472246-cf39-501b-88ba-aea193a2227d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc472246-cf39-501b-88ba-aea193a2227d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d9ed7ec-92aa-541a-9bea-f3cdc0ae0b0e" :answer-field/key "$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae1de41-bd54-5836-8d70-b2f7e421082c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450520} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9484dd-5f72-59ba-b9bf-a220d37834f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cae1de41-bd54-5836-8d70-b2f7e421082c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450520} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad3fb9a-3bbe-53ce-a61d-00b578edb53b" :answer-field/key "$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfb148a-1391-50e5-a4e7-bcf13b54ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450522} {:answer/id #uuid "47fa339f-27e5-5b52-9afc-abbb6a18cfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47fa339f-27e5-5b52-9afc-abbb6a18cfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450523} :answer-field/id #uuid "187f487c-26a4-5852-ba9e-dad42fa9e85d" :answer-field/key "$f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b7e93bb-008a-52c4-ad5d-95180e45a411" :question/math-academy-id "q-338490" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$”; “$f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2da01cc-d314-5f60-8fba-10973e328ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2da01cc-d314-5f60-8fba-10973e328ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310562} :answer-field/id #uuid "35a1764e-e5f6-5095-9b21-9bdf74e71f29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2870fd1f-672a-5c18-b50c-82ac76026af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-338496" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & =\\frac{(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{4x-1}{2x-\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{x})(2x+\\sqrt{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{(2x)^{2}-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{4{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{(4x-1)(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x(4x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{\\cancel{(4x-1)}(2x+\\sqrt{x})}{x\\cancel{(4x-1)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1/4)}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{x}}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314698 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c648e38-7cc5-5dcf-9021-2394fd32b96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312319} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce1fc5f-2da5-5b0b-80ba-f8df3b61c73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312324} {:answer/id #uuid "33913f9d-f88f-57ed-97d8-78055dbc85b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312390} {:answer/id #uuid "744ef512-134b-571f-9ecd-17d43e03b0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312900} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb5671d-5993-5a82-866f-3ac133f5ce78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c648e38-7cc5-5dcf-9021-2394fd32b96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312319} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dfa1250-d18e-541a-ad3a-13bb3e49e2fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "280f32a1-77b0-558e-a146-5c2de5be7ce0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338528" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y={csc}^{3}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x=2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)={csc}^{3}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡2x+2{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡2x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{{csc}^{3}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then $du=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx,$ so $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,du,$ and the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{{sin}^{3}⁡2x}\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}(\\frac{1}{2}\\,du) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-3}\\,du \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{u}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+\\left(-\\frac{1}{8{sin}^{2}⁡2x}\\right)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2x}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}-\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}(2{cos}^{2}⁡2x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,+\\,\\underbrace{\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d24117d-f474-5749-88a8-5583c9294e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d24117d-f474-5749-88a8-5583c9294e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312600} :answer-field/id #uuid "667b194a-703f-5bed-9b6e-abfb678fff9d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314296} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8e717eb-6aa1-5aab-a144-60bac9b6bef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8e717eb-6aa1-5aab-a144-60bac9b6bef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313301} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6d5e605-4dc0-593a-9da8-c2a174173fba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4361090-b0ba-502a-8af2-2c9398f9d515" :question/math-academy-id "q-338573" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ are $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${135}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x={135}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/91/91e4de557177693203cbbc48c12bbf7af3de1739c8405ac05bdfa06d3e92638c.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={225}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-135}^{∘}$ and $x={135}^{∘}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19a030a8-e4a1-54a0-94a1-c323c39238a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19a030a8-e4a1-54a0-94a1-c323c39238a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312162} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b0377b-5695-5fd7-bd06-bd9dd4605ae9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc8a313-3df8-5251-8232-e2779a13fe3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dc8a313-3df8-5251-8232-e2779a13fe3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312606} :answer-field/id #uuid "cabb4c91-0e90-5c57-899c-e9730bf6dc17" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03c93438-78d6-56ca-99d6-08e8278edf47" :question/math-academy-id "q-338574" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a22dba264faa711352724f135d2ec5a074202c4b5069dee2389fe954419a3770.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be06aa4-b76a-5c3d-9413-15a92adc1a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5be06aa4-b76a-5c3d-9413-15a92adc1a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312708} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2c88d65-2a4d-5daa-9ea3-ed26c381629a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314495} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ffc60cf-cf89-5948-b2f6-6162dd5df8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ffc60cf-cf89-5948-b2f6-6162dd5df8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312359} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea5c885-1879-5110-be19-cf81ebc92f8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43e75940-ea81-5b28-a9f4-6ca4e79d3794" :question/math-academy-id "q-338580" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{2},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)=-0.729\\,727…≈-0.7297\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.7297+2\\pi ≈5.5535\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}=5.5535.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$\n\n![](images/48/48d92d2bf48234d7c7aad44936cdefd97f02804383cb7ced2f838661a8c4922a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.7297 \\\\  & ≈3.8713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈3.9,\\,x≈5.6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314707 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f943f84-090b-543a-9c07-3adb3e84dc27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "228.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f943f84-090b-543a-9c07-3adb3e84dc27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "228.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313003} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7efd6f-d23d-5733-95ab-b7e375919a15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb80d9a7-a22d-5752-90b1-c05d63df078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "311.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb80d9a7-a22d-5752-90b1-c05d63df078f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "311.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313854} :answer-field/id #uuid "95941f59-f6b7-527d-bf48-cce303732407" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9df329f0-689c-5480-af86-9fabef73edcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-338585" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3},$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right)={(-48.590\\,377…)}^{∘}≈-{48.5904}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{48.5904}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={311.4096}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={311.4096}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/d3/d319d2ea403460a3b4d86c837a560fd5188476c425399145bb911e1414b0a6f5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{48.5904}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{228.5904}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{228.6}^{∘},\\,x≈{311.4}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e418779-4531-5496-adf6-51a2f6784e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e418779-4531-5496-adf6-51a2f6784e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312989} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d409482-3915-5d87-a1ef-1f1c29041f7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a83efc62-7398-5466-b65f-5821c4945754" :question/math-academy-id "q-338645" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-4y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4.$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4A-4B)-4(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -8B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-4y\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{2}-4A{e}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot {e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is arbitrary."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24d83896-3efd-5741-ad90-6600b09da0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24d83896-3efd-5741-ad90-6600b09da0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312251} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb31bb3-cefc-5270-8eaf-8f0488fff9b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ae5d8ba-d056-56c4-a578-3e324f0f6dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ae5d8ba-d056-56c4-a578-3e324f0f6dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312583} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e99cfe-1ccd-5316-bbb0-4a86ff9a62b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c87c5173-e872-5d2d-993e-17bcdc6ae00d" :question/math-academy-id "q-338688" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order**, the solutions of the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1=0$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/8e/8ea1a5ca6b0dc0bd7867ab841c629f9966f2b97de4585b9c595a33af206c4a7f.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51607053-9c5e-5040-9162-79e7eabe53c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312638} {:answer/id #uuid "83c6d225-b338-5ea6-94ae-e07376b2f80b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313037} {:answer/id #uuid "b996cc77-eae1-514b-bbb0-decff1eedbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51607053-9c5e-5040-9162-79e7eabe53c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312638} :answer-field/id #uuid "1713db3c-3d3b-56d0-9447-0421d904b811" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05981e7e-a6b3-5e22-84c9-8093f732d83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312012} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ff1f02-c8b2-50a8-ae66-6cc69b2f4d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313308} {:answer/id #uuid "ca29fba6-31a6-54d4-88d8-f2df32c3bbf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ff1f02-c8b2-50a8-ae66-6cc69b2f4d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313308} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de04287-b79a-5960-8c3a-b49f84ea4268" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24deef72-aa75-5aec-98ce-143c00ba6af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312252} {:answer/id #uuid "43e04a28-bf22-5c4a-b0c8-43ef774bc699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312521} {:answer/id #uuid "9992402b-1f9d-5969-b149-7699ab0de3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24deef72-aa75-5aec-98ce-143c00ba6af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312252} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9ef1e5-76d5-5faf-bea3-89ed28f9eb06" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314388} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "162f29ce-2b85-58a8-bb17-289e8895f30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312127} {:answer/id #uuid "243acfcc-0ab8-55eb-8306-184ed767cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312246} {:answer/id #uuid "63229f82-b5bf-5d63-85f7-16aec539b6b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Neumann" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312764} {:answer/id #uuid "831c6b05-ce93-5e1b-b767-f293f4fc917b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Robin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243acfcc-0ab8-55eb-8306-184ed767cc0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Dirichlet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312246} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6aabc96-922a-5e8f-9111-640d0e0d8b60" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c856911-b2c8-57b4-a878-13a738162f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-338695" :question/problem "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0\n$$\n\nComplete the missing information below:\n\n$\\,$ The governing differential equation is {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, the {{field-2}} boundary conditions are {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem is {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For a second-order boundary value problem, \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y(x)=f(x),\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x),Q(x)$ and $f(x)$ are all continuous on $[a,b],$ we have the following boundary condition classifications. \n\n- Dirichlet:\n\n$$\ny(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,y(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Neumann:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\n- Robin (mixed):\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)={\\gamma }_{1},\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)={\\gamma }_{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\gamma }_{i},{\\alpha }_{i},$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real numbers with $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ \n\nA set of Dirichlet, Neumann, or Robin boundary conditions is homogeneous if both right-hand sides are zero (i.e., ${\\gamma }_{1}={\\gamma }_{2}=0.)$ Otherwise, they are inhomogeneous.\n\nA boundary value problem is homogeneous if the differential equation is homogeneous $(f≡0)$ and the corresponding boundary conditions are homogeneous. Otherwise, the BVP is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- The governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+x{y}^{′}(x)+(1+{x}^{2})y(x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side $f(x)≡0,$ the equation is $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- The boundary conditions are\n\n$$\ny(0)=0,\\,y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThese are $\\text{Dirichlet}$ boundary conditions, and since the right-hand sides are zero for both, they are $\\text{homogeneous}.$\n\n- Since the governing equation is homogeneous, and the boundary conditions are homogeneous, the BVP is $\\text{homogeneous}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a85177-f8b1-500f-9ba0-3b2f351cdc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04a85177-f8b1-500f-9ba0-3b2f351cdc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311999} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cdf1133-83d7-50c0-b0f3-e3d19aeaa2e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314270} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dac57787-f6fc-5e9c-9491-8258e9b59eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dac57787-f6fc-5e9c-9491-8258e9b59eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313692} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa8900e-0df0-59e7-94e0-7cc4c16b61f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "863434bc-9880-5d8f-880f-7fef27155e0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-338710" :question/problem "Arranged in **ascending order** and rounded to **the nearest integer**, the solutions of the equation $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi $ are $x=${{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{5}{8}\\right)=2.245\\,927…≈2.246\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.246$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is $x=2.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.896.$\n\n![](images/99/992325c7cbf34f4b981af0a9f34740990d39694e727861ca92bf591037436327.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.896 \\\\  & ≈4.038\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi $ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =X-2\\pi  \\\\  & =4.038-2\\pi  \\\\  & ≈-2.245 \\\\  & ≈-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arranged in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314726 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfe98098-6def-5a85-99f7-600e2e7c7c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfe98098-6def-5a85-99f7-600e2e7c7c1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313477} :answer-field/id #uuid "936473fc-6b24-59ab-b5b6-bdfab1892612" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314408} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d75b9e-bdd2-534f-8ff5-8e2740657137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d75b9e-bdd2-534f-8ff5-8e2740657137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313020} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5f5855-f298-502e-898d-56ebc86114f5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57e3ccf9-17c1-5add-96a9-fe13c7b364fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-338723" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-2,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-2 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-5 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)≈-0.9273\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+2\\pi =-0.9273+2\\pi ≈5.3559\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=5.3559.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$\n\n![](images/d8/d8951c588af989cc5a77b213f25a485b8a6e9db1d0f164fc326e65d40c0385c7.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.9273 \\\\  & ≈4.0689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx≈4.1,\\,x≈5.4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f51b4d12-585f-5527-bbf2-fea08f797336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51b4d12-585f-5527-bbf2-fea08f797336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313935} :answer-field/id #uuid "630b3025-13ee-5cc9-9221-4a908b4a749f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314285} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a698ae6-c863-5237-b4d5-2b4214c04e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a698ae6-c863-5237-b4d5-2b4214c04e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "131" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312577} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e6e1b93-6a11-50a3-9e23-8f4526eb9e9b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "42d84ad8-d74a-5e76-9877-3599340a90c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338750" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation  $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=8-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =8-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ 6\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)=(48.590\\,377…)^{∘}≈{48.590}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${48.590}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={48.590}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/b6/b6754300e279bf09409a49aeca7160a319ee90b5a120fe7ac775f12a8444b8af.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{48.590}^{∘} \\\\  & ={131.410}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{49}^{∘},\\,x≈{131}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6a1808-ef55-591e-b8dc-bdd07d336ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "343" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d6a1808-ef55-591e-b8dc-bdd07d336ce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "343" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312979} :answer-field/id #uuid "917db62c-7bcb-527b-ae22-e6d698144440" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314401} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e85f711-74ae-50c0-ba88-d93f8f5a6e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e85f711-74ae-50c0-ba88-d93f8f5a6e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313222} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5f2d02f-029b-52a0-8c37-4d6dd3ea12a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9ef87f8-57b2-52f5-9d33-55ce6c81c20b" :question/math-academy-id "q-338755" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =10 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{10}\\right)=(-17.457\\,603…)^{∘}≈-{17.458}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{17.458}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={342.542}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={342.542}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={17.458}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/82/821713cb7b71b2b15da5ecdf90c0e836234d280fa3753c71615b570017773672.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{17.458}^{∘} \\\\  & ={197.458}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{197}^{∘},\\,x≈{343}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32c3ddef-3866-5229-ba29-2b31c9c090b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "335" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32c3ddef-3866-5229-ba29-2b31c9c090b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "335" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312386} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c75ee6f-80f1-5fe0-a625-24f21068c1a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314268} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8735993a-e142-5f1c-8a06-48cddf61a302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8735993a-e142-5f1c-8a06-48cddf61a302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "205" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313055} :answer-field/id #uuid "90879605-3eae-5799-af76-877b8bae0bbc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "711c7a14-14f8-528d-bec3-d5d76f4ce6c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-338763" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=7+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =7+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{7}\\right)=(-25.376\\,933…)^{∘}≈-{25.377}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{25.377}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={334.623}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={334.623}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={25.377}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={25.377}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/23/2358d2c829f52ba8727afc10a609f923ca441bb55dc398cda78c619454f6eac5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{25.377}^{∘} \\\\  & ={205.377}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{205}^{∘},\\,x≈{335}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6affb839-c6cf-59e6-a967-150ca63c7bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6affb839-c6cf-59e6-a967-150ca63c7bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312815} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f1620a-bff5-5f57-b2e5-c85f03e653b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4833a56a-088c-51e2-9b40-04c820c1773d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4833a56a-088c-51e2-9b40-04c820c1773d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5be4039-8834-5bac-9b7c-2ae0b1fe8350" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "30b736aa-dfe5-5396-8949-80736a2d80b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-338793" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{3}$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)=-0.643\\,501…≈-0.6435\n$$\n\nSince $-0.6435$ lies in the required domain, the first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.6435.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6435.$\n\n![](images/d6/d6fbe829a100e0ee59872f9bc8d2d6501e88b389ada63008fbe1ef6e6eee7ee4.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.6435 \\\\  & ≈3.7851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=3.7851$ is not in the required domain $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi .$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=3.7851-2\\pi ≈-2.4981\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-2.5,\\,x=-0.6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f113be3-23cf-5dfe-89d4-a0c254481d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f113be3-23cf-5dfe-89d4-a0c254481d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312084} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e50ec78-4f66-51cb-9205-1d0962fc2c13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b51a8e37-8366-50cf-ae2d-1383e40b494b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b51a8e37-8366-50cf-ae2d-1383e40b494b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313384} :answer-field/id #uuid "edf0ce8f-8624-5f34-a6e7-8683855f7ca0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07dc3ac4-b444-5ca0-ad53-3c75d749d994" :question/math-academy-id "q-338798" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\sqrt{2}$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/01/012f0986293e2a4318dc68633a4ac0e91732d74300496427aaaa6635b417efe4.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bede224-8f50-5aa0-8b2b-a801cede1199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bede224-8f50-5aa0-8b2b-a801cede1199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312968} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a5b00d1-2e6e-57d6-90cf-1034596a8657" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314379} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f24a79e-4055-5bfd-a351-f36b5038f633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f24a79e-4055-5bfd-a351-f36b5038f633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312350} :answer-field/id #uuid "986e6fbc-abdb-59c4-9841-b67138da825b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f6951732-7ef2-5d8b-9bcb-747d15e6a865" :question/math-academy-id "q-338802" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3},$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the given equation and isolate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/fd/fd0cf34edebb21a059d60bb3588b27df2da4c86de0bdca044c0e702d9e239d7b.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={60}^{∘},\\,x={120}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aedd9bd-ed1a-5e77-b2f7-f30da0a5f713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312306} {:answer/id #uuid "57ccbd57-fa23-559b-98b8-b5e841180c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312681} {:answer/id #uuid "cdef54e5-6300-5adf-8b67-f67f3d6adf19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313582} {:answer/id #uuid "e84e2fcb-966f-50fe-933a-7d7bca5b43cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e84e2fcb-966f-50fe-933a-7d7bca5b43cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313823} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c06983b-ddc0-5fb8-876c-9a1b2e5a5d20" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314226} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e2552c-69a6-579e-bbf1-16c8783aa693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-\\frac{y}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312231} {:answer/id #uuid "570993af-0477-5d4a-a51a-93ab09600014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312675} {:answer/id #uuid "8abc34ec-135c-55d0-9650-b60ad2fb71aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313078} {:answer/id #uuid "fbced680-9552-56f6-9aa0-2c4c50f109b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}-y=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8abc34ec-135c-55d0-9650-b60ad2fb71aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313078} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b72e68-7474-5f06-9316-c9731a3be22e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e13738f-f5b5-5418-9627-22b39e555f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338870" :question/problem "Consider the inexact differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$\n\nIt is known that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}\n$$\n\nwhere the functions $P$ and $Q$ are given by \n\n$$\nP(x,y)={x}^{2}y,\\,Q(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUse this information to find a suitable integrating factor $I(y)$ and hence find the general solution to the equation.\n\n$\\,$ A suitable integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." :question/worked-solution "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 2/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\right)}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}+y)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3c72a7-9815-516b-abd7-ac00d7d82af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a3c72a7-9815-516b-abd7-ac00d7d82af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313074} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c18ae5f-a121-51ad-9c67-21aad4e4fdfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5553df31-6113-5f5b-a73a-06eefe35a840" :question/math-academy-id "q-339080" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {7}^{-x}) & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({7}^{-1})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2858b7d6adc07c0f2196bbe8170f17fa02aff19d494726c08f7c03a419afd53.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function approaches zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)}^{x}=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b45999fc-bb4c-515a-895e-03b290fee928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b45999fc-bb4c-515a-895e-03b290fee928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313378} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d01ef8-0c95-50ce-8bf5-578ebf15999b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b46ef53-9e9f-5f4a-9061-327f429a33fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-339131" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](images/85/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =2\\sqrt{3}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)+6\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3+6\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =3+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bee4f3-b655-5e19-8244-51de7d7873c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.1$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311981} {:answer/id #uuid "825af481-1e71-5353-9701-9b79d446e326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.2$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313025} {:answer/id #uuid "98974d5d-404e-5347-bde0-987fd09ec5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313184} {:answer/id #uuid "dbded73a-35b2-5b76-9303-73936db48bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.4$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313706} {:answer/id #uuid "e5672fee-4675-514e-b601-1ada102f0be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.5$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98974d5d-404e-5347-bde0-987fd09ec5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.8$ years" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313184} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbdadf62-38ec-57ce-bd56-7f4c297a1eb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b71a4a6b-7596-5bb0-a385-748571c7e195" :question/math-academy-id "q-339219" :question/problem "The price $P(t)$ of a particular good varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in years, and $r>0.$ It is known that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. How long did it take for the price to double from the initial price?" :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the price of the good when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the price equals $2{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =2{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=2{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the price of the good tripled after the first six years. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(6)=3{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{6r}=3{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{6r} & =3{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{6r} & =3\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{6r} & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{6r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈3.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e61fea9-46c4-571c-af25-0b4b8d2233e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.6$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312339} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc1ff51-5a3a-5093-854b-97d69e9689ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.4$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312354} {:answer/id #uuid "36d8246d-2859-53c5-bd0c-93a6a1e60033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.5$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312416} {:answer/id #uuid "40eba7b4-5d78-5d84-810b-a485aa1dfd2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.8$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312495} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a4eb57-5d98-50c6-a832-625ce8d73b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.7$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e61fea9-46c4-571c-af25-0b4b8d2233e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.6$ months" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312339} :answer-field/id #uuid "2892f9f8-27d6-5f43-8b29-32d833188429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30f450e1-9f50-50d0-a5b8-9d9411257241" :question/math-academy-id "q-339223" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of mice in a forest varies according to the differential equation ${P}^{′}(t)=rP(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in months from the start of the observation period, and $r>0.$  It is known that the mouse population quintuples (increases fivefold) after the first two months. How long does it take for the mouse population to increase eightfold from the start of the observation period? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}{e}^{rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ is the mouse population when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the mouse population equals $8{P}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =8{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}}=8{P}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{r{t}_{1}} & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ r{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the population quintuples after the first two months. Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(2)=5{P}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{P}_{0}{e}^{2r}=5{P}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{0}{e}^{2r} & =5{P}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{P}_{0}}{e}^{2r} & =5\\cancel{{P}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{2r} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ r & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & ≈2.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314758 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f80ff36-5f4b-50b2-82c2-4245585a8511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.7$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312852} {:answer/id #uuid "72d8a0ef-a660-50d1-8a17-b313b5e65157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312882} {:answer/id #uuid "8f244e3a-fd2e-548e-b768-a48abde793be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313117} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc5130c-2075-5be4-bdb6-de43f8dfa703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313122} {:answer/id #uuid "f83f564a-cdd1-5464-a4c4-4004788eb34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d8a0ef-a660-50d1-8a17-b313b5e65157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.6$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312882} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9c2d1b-c0ae-5a85-b5e5-e995c225e2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3648d5b-2812-593c-b336-3a42fac9c75a" :question/math-academy-id "q-339228" :question/problem "A car is traveling along a straight road to a particular destination. The remaining distance $D(t)$ varies according to the differential equation ${D}^{′}(t)=-rD(t),$ where $t$ is the time, measured in hours, and $r>0.$ Two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. How long does it take for the remaining distance to reduce to one-third of the journey's total distance? Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nD(t)={D}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${D}_{0}$ is the distance when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the distance equals $\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r}\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that two hours into the journey, the remaining distance is three-quarters of the journey's total distance. Therefore,\n\n$$\nD(2)=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}\\,⇒\\,{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r}=\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{0}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}{D}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{D}_{0}}{e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cancel{{D}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-2r} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-2r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\ -2r & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) \\\\ r & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(∗)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)} \\\\  & ≈7.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314769 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e3d989-efe2-5099-9a9b-85e7153a3b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35e3d989-efe2-5099-9a9b-85e7153a3b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b165d46c-7ad2-56cc-888a-401d7c0e640d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e75bdea-4506-5a3d-8157-9fb9c7f2fd37" :question/math-academy-id "q-339311" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Suppose that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5.5}^{2})$ and $P(X\\le 18.2)=0.5871.$ Find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 18.2) & =0.5871 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}) & =0.5871 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5}\\right) & =0.5871 \\\\ \\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.22)=0.5871\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5871)=0.22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18.2-\\mu }{5.5} & =0.22 \\\\ 18.2-\\mu  & =1.21 \\\\ \\mu  & =16.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17135ecd-8533-5d0e-9851-09a82d7e3a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17135ecd-8533-5d0e-9851-09a82d7e3a4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312138} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6d6473e-5676-5c13-b96f-90865b3ddc82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "821c3a6e-2859-5147-bbd0-a91716f2cba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339330" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1):$\n\n![](images/e4/e441953c2cefc6f817a64cb7ce0ebce53164ebb4d3974ae871b14d57450b5167.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }(\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x+1))=\\pi -\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8af64ba-f686-5f2c-86c2-0ee5846198a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8af64ba-f686-5f2c-86c2-0ee5846198a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313962} :answer-field/id #uuid "a933c97d-3266-504d-bef7-e1c56e2aebed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a48ac92-eea9-5a99-a96b-31703706f874" :question/math-academy-id "q-339380" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5):$ \n\n![](images/25/25cea33f2fc3a3bcdbc10ca03f93bd6059eb7d0a7dfcda5eb59f6f671387aea3.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5))=2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bb95a1b-c69a-5c85-ba30-c6dfde434d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bb95a1b-c69a-5c85-ba30-c6dfde434d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312588} :answer-field/id #uuid "4089e77e-6875-5a03-89a2-c9b816b75ffb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14adae41-e106-531a-960b-9ce7071cabb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339406" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}^{x} & =\\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/9a/9a249437f97a441c409b17e0f30a59bdcb70df02610f18d9320361d3d0d6708a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{7}\\right)}^{x}=\\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right)\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb130041-a33a-543e-a733-2be46e7c69c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb130041-a33a-543e-a733-2be46e7c69c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313980} :answer-field/id #uuid "965b38d0-d51e-5cc3-97d0-841b6c3f10cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ff15497-b2ec-532a-981c-c68f8be3bb35" :question/math-academy-id "q-339407" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/99/99c2329c99005af33f4b62d61547e18dcaa7f04afc931df57439bd1ca08ff35c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "390133c1-c9a0-518b-a603-5b9b7e8510ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4s}{5}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312437} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe79f03-6019-5209-b2d4-236e7ccea6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{3}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312489} {:answer/id #uuid "4d22c93e-8539-5fe4-843e-30c08ac16782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{2s}{5}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312599} {:answer/id #uuid "78d21419-b604-5de8-943f-39fd19b2df7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312938} {:answer/id #uuid "d0468ebe-2044-55f6-b33c-730b38ac234b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4s}{5}\\,i+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d21419-b604-5de8-943f-39fd19b2df7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "T(s)=\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312938} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae6bebbc-0cf8-5a0f-afb0-2e619117694a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e98268a-2c12-50af-adfa-eb48d558ff91" :question/math-academy-id "q-33984" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=8t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡6t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡6t\\,k,$ $t\\ge 0,$ for which the arc length $s$ from $r(0)$ to $r(t)$ is given by $s=10t,$ find the corresponding unit tangent $T(s),$ parametrized by $s.$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $s=10t$, we have that $t=\\frac{s}{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the vector function $R(s)$ that parametrizes $r(t)$ by its arc length is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(s) & =r\\left(\\frac{s}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\left(\\frac{s}{10}\\right)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{6s}{10}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6s}{10}\\right)k \\\\  & =\\frac{4s}{5}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since the curve is parametrized by its arc length $s$, the unit tangent can be found as\n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s).\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}R(s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4s}{5}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))k \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
